# Brain Blocklist — PRIVATE (never commit this file)
#
# Where it lives: ~/.claude/company/brain-blocklist.txt
# What it does:   scripts/check-generic.py reads this and blocks any commit
#                 whose message or staged files mention these tokens.
#
# Why it matters: ~/.claude/ is an open-source repo. Its git log is public.
#                 The framework is generic by design — arm codes, client
#                 names, coworkers, internal projects, vendor incidents,
#                 ticket numbers MUST NOT leak into git history.
#
# Format:
#   • One token per line.
#   • Comments start with '#'.
#   • Tokens are matched case-insensitively as whole words (\b boundaries).
#   • Multi-word tokens are matched literally (e.g. "client one project x").
#   • Avoid generic English words — they'd produce constant false positives.
#
# How to use:
#   1. Copy this file:  cp templates/company/brain-blocklist.txt.template \
#                          company/brain-blocklist.txt
#   2. Replace the placeholders below with YOUR sensitive tokens.
#   3. Add new tokens whenever you onboard a new arm or partner.
#
# Suggested categories (delete what doesn't apply, add what does):

# --- Arm codes (your private project shortnames) ---
# arm-code-one
# arm-code-two

# --- Client / partner names ---
# client-org-name
# partner-vendor-name

# --- People (operators, coworkers, contacts you correspond about) ---
# given-name-coworker
# given-name-partner

# --- Internal project codenames ---
# internal-codename-alpha
# internal-codename-beta

# --- Vendor incidents / ticket references ---
# specific-vendor-incident-id
# internal-ticket-prefix

# --- Anything else you'd be uncomfortable seeing in a public git log ---
